data.message:
Two problems:

1. **extraction.ts errors**: `r` is typed as `Record<string, unknown>` — accessing `.title` etc. triggers TS4111 (property from index signature must be accessed with ['title']). I need to change `const r = raw as Record<string, unknown>` and use bracket access, or cast differently. Let me use a helper that extracts safely with bracket access.

2. **index.ts errors**: The `requests` object no longer has its `requests` wrapper typing. Previously it was `requests: {...}` inside `handlers: { requests: ... }` where the whole thing was `BrowserView.defineRPC<TrailViewerRPC>` — the contextually-typed object. Now that `requests` is hoisted as a standalone const, TypeScript infers its type from the object literal — the handler params lose contextual typing (they were typed by `TrailViewerRequests`). So `({ id })` params become implicitly `any`.

I need to type the `requests` const explicitly. The cleanest: annotate `const requests: TrailViewerRequests = { ... }` — but the handlers are async functions returning promises, whereas the contract types define `params`/`response` shapes, not function types. The contract `TrailViewerRequests` is `{ listTabs: { params; response }; ... }` — NOT the handler signature. The defineRPC wrapped them. So I can't just annotate with TrailViewerRequests.

I need to reconstruct the typing. The original `defineRPC<TrailViewerRPC>` contextually typed handlers. Without the wrapper, I lose it. Options:

a) Type the handlers object with a mapped type derived from TrailViewerRequests:
```ts
type RequestHandlers = {
  [K in keyof TrailViewerRequests]: (params: TrailViewerRequests[K]["params"]) => TrailViewerRequests[K]["response"] | Promise<TrailViewerRequests[K]["response"]>;
};
const requests: RequestHandlers = { ... };
```
This gives contextual typing to each handler's params. 

But wait — does defineRPC's expected handlers type match this exactly? If `requests` is typed as `RequestHandlers`, then `handlers: { requests }` — the defineRPC generic `TrailViewerRPC` expects `requests` to be the handler-map type. If my `RequestHandlers` structurally matches, it's fine. Let me check what type defineRPC expects. It's from electrobun. The generic `TrailViewerRPC` = `{ bun: { requests: TrailViewerRequests; messages: TrailViewerMessages } }`. defineRPC presumably derives handler types from this. My `RequestHandlers` should be assignable.

Let me define it right before `const requests`:
```ts
type RequestHandler = {
  [K in keyof TrailViewerRequests]: (
    params: TrailViewerRequests[K]["params"],
  ) => TrailViewerRequests[K]["response"] | Promise<TrailViewerRequests[K]["response"]>;
};

const requests: RequestHandler = {
  ...
};
```

That should fix the implicit-any errors in index.ts.

But also — the `getSessionEvents` handler response: the contract response is `{ ok: boolean; error?: string; events?: SessionEventRow[]; repoRoot?: string; repos?: RepoInfo[]; session?: {...} }`. The handler returns exactly that. Good. But
…[+15 chars]

toolInput.newString:
function validateCard(
	raw: unknown,
	idx: number,
): ConceptCardData | null {
	if (typeof raw !== "object" || raw === null) return null;
	const r = raw as Record<string, unknown>;
	const title =
		typeof r["title"] === "string" && (r["title"] as string).trim()
			? (r["title"] as string).trim()
			: "";
	const description =
		typeof r["description"] === "string" && (r["description"] as string).trim()
			? (r["description"] as string).trim()
			: "";
	if (!title || !description) return null;

	const changeType = VALID_CHANGE_TYPES.includes(
		r["changeType"] as ConceptChangeType,
	)
		? (r["changeType"] as ConceptChangeType)
		: "execution";
	const points = Array.isArray(r["points"])
		? (r["points"] as unknown[]).filter((p): p is string => typeof p === "string")
		: [];
	const sessionIds = Array.isArray(r["sessionIds"])
		? (r["sessionIds"] as unknown[]).filter(
				(s): s is string => typeof s === "string",
			)
		: [];
	// Card repos are owner/name pairs — entries without an owner (local repos)
	// are dropped rather than carried as nulls.
	const repos = Array.isArray(r["repos"])
		? (r["repos"] as unknown[])
				.filter(
					(re): re is { owner?: unknown; name?: unknown } =>
						typeof re === "object" && re !== null,
				)
				.map((re) => ({
					owner: typeof re.owner === "string" ? re.owner : "",
					name: typeof re.name === "string" ? re.name : "",
				}))
				.filter((re) => re.owner !== "" && re.name !== "")
		: [];
	return {
		id:
			typeof r["id"] === "string" && (r["id"] as string).trim()
				? (r["id"] as string).trim()
				: `concept-${idx}`,
		title,
		changeType,
		status: "draft",
		sessionIds,
		repos,
		description,
		points: points.length > 0 ? points : [title],
		mermaid:
			typeof r["mermaid"] === "string" && (r["mermaid"] as string).trim()
				? (r["mermaid"] as string).trim()
				: "",
	};
}

